Not all randomly generated monsters are so easily dealt with as a
pudding.  Some do not obey E; some will summon other nasty monsters.  At
least two of those summoners are not genocidable.

When your level is full of puddings, however, random monster generation
slows to a trickle, and those monsters who do appear tend to show up far
enough away from you that you have no fear from them -- they can't get
through the puddings to reach you.

In addition, pudding farming can (and has been) automated on
nethack.alt.org (as has Rider farming, though the effort required for
the setup on the latter trick is noticeably larger).  When it is
possible can sit and repeatedly paste a text string into a terminal and
pretend that you're "playing" the game, the flaw has passed beyond
trivial and is now just stupid.

To my knowledge, nobody has successfully figured out a means to sit and
automate the "don ring of slow digestion, wait for monsters to spawn,
and deal with them all effectively" tactic.  The disparity in effort is,
of course, obvious.

> seems no different than pudding farming.  It seems like the potential
> to gain infinite amounts of loot through less-than-glamourous means
> was actually an intentional "feature" rather than a "flaw".

The potential to wander the dungeon endlessly, kill things, and pick up
loot is certainly a feature.  But comparing the effort required to
wander the dungeon and kill random monsters to the effort required to
pudding farm is taking the "playing dumb" tactic to the extreme; it's
apples to kittens, plain and simple.

